<h2>Why this little case is a useful map</h2>
<p>Creative-economy partnerships — photography studios, content agencies, boutique production houses — increasingly organise as LLPs, with the brand, the domains and the Instagram page as the real capital. When partners fall out, three questions arrive together: who controls the brand while the fight lasts, who guards the bank account, and which forum decides. This order answers all three within the arbitration framework.</p>

<h2>The Section 9 stage: holding the ring</h2>
<p>At the August 2026 hearing the Court found the petitioner had a direct, contractual and beneficial interest in preserving the LLP&rsquo;s business and assets, and that the limited interim protection sought was against dissipation and misuse pending adjudication. The prima facie reading of the LLP agreement supplied the legal spine:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Clause</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Clause 41 (arbitration)</td><td>All partner and partner-LLP disputes under the agreement go to arbitration under the 1996 Act</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Clause 13 (independent business)</td><td>Permitted — but only with prior intimation to the LLP, without using the LLP&rsquo;s name, and subject to Clause 14</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Clause 14 (non-compete)</td><td>Prohibits directly competing activity; profits of any such activity must be transferred to the LLP</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Asset-protection clauses</td><td>Separately prohibit misuse of the LLP&rsquo;s information and assets</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The alleged transfers totalling Rs 53.26 lakh from the LLP&rsquo;s account to a partner&rsquo;s personal account, and the creation of a parallel domain carrying the disputed brand, were precisely the kind of asset-dissipation and goodwill-erosion concerns Section 9 exists to address.</p>

<h2>The Section 11 stage: a tribunal with a timetable</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Mediation first, arbitration ready.</strong> The arbitrator enters reference three weeks from the mediation-failure report — a sequencing that keeps settlement alive without letting the reference drift.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Institutional administration.</strong> DIAC rules and fee schedule govern — Delhi&rsquo;s default choice for court-appointed references, giving the parties case management and fee predictability.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>All objections preserved.</strong> Arbitrability, jurisdiction and limitation pleas remain open before the tribunal — the appointment decides nothing on merits.</div>
</div>

<h2>The conversion direction: Section 9 to Section 17</h2>
<p>Once a tribunal is constituted, Section 9(3) makes the court a reluctant forum for interim measures: the tribunal&rsquo;s Section 17 power is the primary one. The order operationalises this cleanly — the pending Section 9 petition converts into a Section 17 application, the existing interim arrangement continues until the arbitrator revisits it, and the tribunal is at liberty to confirm, modify or vacate. Parties get continuity of protection without parallel forums.</p>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Draft LLP agreements with the fight in mind.</strong> An arbitration clause, a clear non-compete, and express brand-ownership recitals turn a chaotic falling-out into a structured reference.</p>
  <p><strong>Move Section 9 with documents.</strong> Bank statements showing transfers, WHOIS records for rival domains, and screenshots of platform takeovers made this petitioner&rsquo;s case concrete.</p>
  <p><strong>Expect the handover.</strong> Interim relief won in court will be re-examined by the tribunal; build the Section 17 record accordingly.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises orders of the Delhi High Court for general information. It is not legal advice; interim relief and appointment outcomes depend on each agreement and record.</div>
